16. Glossary
Term Plain meaning
Congestion Packets arrive faster than the link can send, so
queues fill and overflow
Queue Waiting room in a router; drop-tail means
newcomers are dropped when it is full
RTT Round trip time: send to ACK
BDP Rate × RTT: packets that fit in the pipe
cwnd How many packets I'm allowed in flight now
ssthresh Where growth changes from fast to slow
pipe Packets I think are in the network right now
Slow start cwnd += 1 per ACK, doubling per RTT
Congestion avoidance cwnd += 1/cwnd per ACK, +1 per RTT
AIMD Additive increase, multiplicative decrease
RTO Retransmission timeout
Karn's rule Don't take RTT samples from retransmitted
packets
Backoff Double the RTO after a timeout
Loss event One congestion incident, which may drop many
packets
ACK clocking Each ACK lets one new packet out, so the sending
rate follows the delivery rate
Bufferbloat Oversized queues adding delay
Flow control Protects the receiver, unlike congestion control
which protects the network
