cwnd adapts, and it needs no knowledge of the network.
15. Weaknesses of what we have (and what step 5 fixes)
1. Detection is slow. A loss is noticed only after a full 
rto , and meanwhile 
cwnd keeps
growing (that is the overshoot to 65).
2. The crash to 1 is too harsh. After a timeout the window restarts from 1, which wastes
time. A timeout means "nothing is getting through", but a burst of drops in the queue
still leaves many packets getting through.
3. Random loss is misread. Every loss is treated as congestion, so a lossy but
uncongested link would make 
cwnd stay tiny.
4. Only one flow was tested. Fairness between several senders isn't tried yet.
Step 5 fixes the first two. If the receiver has acked packets sent after packet N, but N is
still unacked, then N is almost certainly lost, because later packets overtook it. QUIC's rule
is a threshold of 3 later packets acked, and TCP's equivalent is 3 duplicate ACKs. The
sender can then detect loss in about one RTT, not a full timeout. And because ACKs are
still arriving, the network is clearly still working, so the sender only does 
ssthresh =
cwnd/2; cwnd = ssthresh . It halves instead of crashing to 1. Timeouts stay as a last
resort for the case where nothing comes back. The result is a smoother sawtooth with
higher throughput, like real TCP.
