Congestion Control, From the Start
Congestion Control, From the Start
A beginner's guide, based on the reliable-UDP project (relay, RTT estimator and 
sender)
1. The problem
cwnd
Imagine a one-lane road. Cars (packets) arrive from many places, and the road can carry
only so many per second. If more cars arrive than the road can carry, they wait in a line
(the queue). The line has a limited length. When it is full, new cars are turned away
(dropped).
Your sender can't see the road, the line, or the other cars. All it knows is:
I sent a packet.
Its ACK came back (all is well), or it didn't (something went wrong).
If every sender just sends as fast as it can, the queues overflow, packets are dropped, and
every sender retransmits the drops. That adds more traffic, which causes more drops. The
network spends its time carrying copies of lost packets and delivers almost nothing
useful. This is called congestion collapse, and it really happened on the early Internet in
1986.
Congestion control is the sender's job of finding out how fast it can send without
overloading the network, using only the ACKs it gets back, and adjusting continuously,
because the answer keeps changing (other senders start and stop).
