Page 1 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
The sliding window is the mechanism. Congestion control decides how big the window is
right now. Before, you picked a number (
WINDOW = 8). Now the sender chooses it itself
while running.
3. The numbers that describe a link
Your relay has these settings. Each one is a real concept.
Rate (bandwidth): 200 packets/s. One packet takes 1/200 = 5 ms to push onto the
link.
Delay: 20 ms each way, so the RTT (round-trip time) is about 40 ms. Add the 5 ms
transmit time and the measured 
srtt is about 46 ms.
Queue: 20 packets of waiting room.
Bandwidth-delay product (BDP) = rate × RTT = 200 × 0.04 = 8 packets. This is how many
packets fit "inside the pipe" at once. Think of a water pipe: its length is the delay, its width
is the rate, and the volume is the BDP.
What happens with different windows:
Packets in flight
Result
Less than 8
About 8
8 to 28
The pipe isn't full, so you waste capacity. You are
slower than you could be.
The pipe is exactly full. Best speed, empty queue,
lowest RTT.
The extra packets wait in the queue. Same speed,
but every packet is delayed. RTT rises.
More than 28 (8 + 20) The queue is full. Packets are dropped.
So this relay's capacity is about 28 packets. Remember that number: it is where the
sawtooth peaks later. The sender doesn't know it, so it has to find it.
4. The core idea: probe and back off
The sender can't ask the network "how fast may I go?". So it behaves like this:
1. Send a little, and it works.
Page 2 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
2. Send a little more, and it still works.
3. Keep increasing until something is lost. That means it went too far.
4. Cut back sharply.
5. Start increasing again.
It never settles, because it keeps testing for more capacity. That is also how it notices
when capacity appears (another sender stopped) or disappears (a new one started).
5. The loss signal
The sender uses a missing ACK as the sign of congestion: a packet unacked for longer
than the timeout is treated as lost, and lost means "I'm sending too fast".
This is an assumption, and sometimes it is wrong. A drop could come from a corrupted
packet or a flaky wireless link, not congestion. That is why the sender's 
stay at 0 in these experiments: random loss would make --loss should
cwnd shrink for no reason.
6. The variables
cwnd (congestion window): how many packets the sender may have in the network
right now. It starts at 1 and changes all the time. It is a float because it grows by
fractions.
ssthresh (slow start threshold): a remembered boundary. Below it the sender grows
fast, above it grows slowly. It starts huge (1000) and is set when a loss happens.
pipe : how many packets the sender believes are currently in the network, meaning
sent, not acked, and not already considered lost. The sender may send only while
pipe < cwnd .
MAX_CWND : a hard cap of 200, so the sender never exceeds the receiver's window of
256.
7. Slow start: growing fast
Start with 
cwnd = 1. The rule is: for every ACK received, 
Round trip
