Question it answers
In your project
How can I keep many
packets in flight and still
be reliable?
Your SR code, per-packet
ACKs and timers
Can the receiver hold
what I send?
Receiver 
WINDOW = 256 ,
sender 
MAX_CWND = 200
The network in
between
Can the routers handle
how fast I send?
cwnd , the new part
Page 1 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
The sliding window is the mechanism. Congestion control decides how big the window is
right now. Before, you picked a number (
WINDOW = 8). Now the sender chooses it itself
while running.
3. The numbers that describe a link
Your relay has these settings. Each one is a real concept.
Rate (bandwidth): 200 packets/s. One packet takes 1/200 = 5 ms to push onto the
link.
Delay: 20 ms each way, so the RTT (round-trip time) is about 40 ms. Add the 5 ms
transmit time and the measured 
srtt is about 46 ms.
Queue: 20 packets of waiting room.
Bandwidth-delay product (BDP) = rate × RTT = 200 × 0.04 = 8 packets. This is how many
packets fit "inside the pipe" at once. Think of a water pipe: its length is the delay, its width
is the rate, and the volume is the BDP.
What happens with different windows:
Packets in flight
Result
Less than 8
About 8
8 to 28
The pipe isn't full, so you waste capacity. You are
slower than you could be.
The pipe is exactly full. Best speed, empty queue,
lowest RTT.
The extra packets wait in the queue. Same speed,
but every packet is delayed. RTT rises.
More than 28 (8 + 20) The queue is full. Packets are dropped.
So this relay's capacity is about 28 packets. Remember that number: it is where the
sawtooth peaks later. The sender doesn't know it, so it has to find it.
4. The core idea: probe and back off
The sender can't ask the network "how fast may I go?". So it behaves like this:
1. Send a little, and it works.
Page 2 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
2. Send a little more, and it still works.
3. Keep increasing until something is lost. That means it went too far.
4. Cut back sharply.
5. Start increasing again.
It never settles, because it keeps testing for more capacity. That is also how it notices
when capacity appears (another sender stopped) or disappears (a new one started).
5. The loss signal
The sender uses a missing ACK as the sign of congestion: a packet unacked for longer
than the timeout is treated as lost, and lost means "I'm sending too fast".
This is an assumption, and sometimes it is wrong. A drop could come from a corrupted
packet or a flaky wireless link, not congestion. That is why the sender's 
stay at 0 in these experiments: random loss would make --loss should
cwnd shrink for no reason.
6. The variables
cwnd (congestion window): how many packets the sender may have in the network
right now. It starts at 1 and changes all the time. It is a float because it grows by
fractions.
ssthresh (slow start threshold): a remembered boundary. Below it the sender grows
fast, above it grows slowly. It starts huge (1000) and is set when a loss happens.
pipe : how many packets the sender believes are currently in the network, meaning
sent, not acked, and not already considered lost. The sender may send only while
pipe < cwnd .
MAX_CWND : a hard cap of 200, so the sender never exceeds the receiver's window of
256.
7. Slow start: growing fast
Start with 
cwnd = 1. The rule is: for every ACK received, 
Round trip
1
In flight
ACKs returned
1
1
cwnd += 1.
cwnd after
2
Page 3 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
Round trip
2
3
4
In flight
2
ACKs returned
2
4
4
8
8
cwnd after
4
8
16
It doubles every RTT, which is exponential growth. The name is misleading: it is "slow"
only compared with blasting at full speed from the very start, which is what the first
protocols did. This phase lasts while 
cwnd < ssthresh .
8. Congestion avoidance: growing carefully
When 
cwnd reaches 
ssthresh , the sender is near the level where it last had trouble, so it
slows down. The rule is: for every ACK, 
cwnd += 1/cwnd .
If 
cwnd = 10, one RTT brings about 10 ACKs, and each adds 0.1, so 
cwnd grows by about 1
packet per RTT. That is a straight, gentle line instead of a curve. It is called additive
increase.
9. Reacting to loss: halve and restart
When loss is detected:
1. 
ssthresh = cwnd / 2. Remember that half of where we were is a safe level. This is
multiplicative decrease.
2. 
cwnd = 1. After a timeout the sender doesn't trust the network at all, so it starts over
with slow start, which climbs quickly back to 
ssthresh (the new, lower boundary)
and then goes gently.
Slow increase plus fast decrease is called AIMD. It is used because it is stable and fair. If
two senders share a link and one has more, the cuts hurt the bigger one more (halving
takes more away) while both gain the same +1 per RTT, so over time they converge to
equal shares.
Page 4 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
10. One loss event, one cut
When a queue overflows it usually drops many packets in a burst, say 10 in a row. If the
sender halved 
ssthresh for each one, it would cut ten times for a single event and
collapse to nothing.
The code uses 
recovery_end . When it cuts, it records 
next_seq , the number of the next
packet that hasn't been sent yet. Later, a lost packet with a number below that was sent
before the cut, so it belongs to the same event, and it only gets retransmitted. Only a loss
of a packet sent after the cut counts as a new event and causes another cut.
11. Timeouts: when is a packet lost?
Congestion control reacts to loss, so detecting loss quickly and accurately matters. That
is the job of the RTT estimator (step 3).
RTT sample: time from sending a packet to receiving its ACK.
srtt : a smoothed average. Each new sample moves it only 1/8 of the way, so one odd
sample doesn't matter.
rttvar : how much the RTT jumps around. Steady RTT means a tight timeout. Jumpy
RTT means more margin.
rto = 
srtt + 4 × rttvar: the timeout. A packet unacked for longer than this is
declared lost.
Karn's rule: if a packet was retransmitted, you can't tell which copy an ACK answers,
so don't use it as a sample. The code only samples packets with 
Backoff: on a timeout, 
retries == 0 .
rto doubles (up to 2 s), because a timeout suggests the
network is struggling. The next good sample brings it back.
A fixed timeout can't work here. As the queue fills, the RTT grows from 47 ms to 140 ms.
Too short a timeout would declare slow packets lost, which makes more traffic and more
congestion. Too long a timeout would react slowly.
12. How the SR bookkeeping fits
This is the part that is easy to get wrong, so here are the exact rules in the code.
lost is a set of packets that timed out and still need to be retransmitted.
Each time around the main loop:
Page 5 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
1. Send step: while 
pipe < cwnd , send. Retransmissions of 
lost packets (lowest
number first) go before new data. Every send does 
pipe += 1.
2. ACK step: read ACKs. For each new one:
mark it acked and do 
pipe -= 1
take an RTT sample (if never retransmitted)
grow 
cwnd (slow start or avoidance)
slide 
base forward over consecutive acked packets
3. Timeout step: every unacked packet older than 
lost and 
rto is marked lost, so it is added to
pipe -= 1 (it is presumably no longer in the network). If it is a new loss
event, 
ssthresh = cwnd/2 and 
cwnd = 1.
Why retransmissions are limited by 
cwnd too: suppose 20 packets time out together. If
the sender resent all 20 at once, it would flood the queue again right after cutting the
window. Counting them in 
pipe means that with 
cwnd = 1, it resends one, waits for its
ACK, then two, then four, and so on, so recovery uses slow start as well.
This also explains ACK clocking: each returning ACK frees one slot in 
pipe , which lets
the sender send exactly one new packet. The sending rate automatically matches the rate
the network is delivering, without any timer.
13. Reading the plot
First climb (0 to 0.5 s): 
cwnd doubles each RTT up to 65. The queue was already
overflowing at about 28, but the sender doesn't know until a timeout fires, about 2
RTTs later, and 
cwnd keeps doubling during that wait. That overshoot is why the first
peak is so high.
First cut: 
ssthresh = 65 / 2 = 32.5, 
Later cycles: 
cwnd = 1. It climbs fast again.
cwnd rises along a gentle line to about 30, which matches the capacity
of 8 (pipe) + 20 (queue) = 28. Then a cut, then back to slow start up to 
ssthresh
(about 15), then a gentle line again. The "sawtooth" is the sender repeatedly finding
the limit.
Bottom panel (
srtt ): it rises from 47 ms to about 140 ms as the queue fills (20
packets × 5 ms = 100 ms of waiting added), then falls after each cut when the queue
drains. This is bufferbloat: big queues add delay.
Page 6 of 9
Congestion Control, From the Start
14. Why the results look the way they do
